        "comments": "Helicobacter canadensis MIT 98-5491 Sequence Consortium is comprised of the University of Birmingham, UK, Baylor College of Medicine, Texas, USA and the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine, UK. Genome sequenced with 454 FLX single- and paired-end libraries to an average coverage depth of 100x with an average paired-end insert length of 2.5kb. Illumina Genetic Analyzer 1 (Solexa) single-end data was also obtained and used to help verify base calls, particularly in regions containing homopolymers.\nSource DNA and bacteria available from J. Dennis Linton (James.D.Linton@manchester.ac.uk)\nGenome coverage: 100x\nSequencing Technology: 454; Solexa\nHelicobacter canadensis MIT 98-5491 Sequence Consortium is comprised of the University of Birmingham, UK, Baylor College of Medicine, Texas, USA and the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine, UK. Genome sequenced with 454 FLX single- and paired-end libraries to an average coverage depth of 100x with an average paired-end insert length of 2.5kb. Illumina Genetic Analyzer 1 (Solexa) single-end data was also obtained and used to help verify base calls, particularly in regions containing homopolymers.\nSource DNA and bacteria available from J. Dennis Linton (James.D.Linton@manchester.ac.uk)\nGenome coverage: 100x\nSequencing Technology: 454; Solexa\n\nThe first and last contigs are contiguous. A single contig was split into two, ACSF01000001 and ACSF01000004, in order to make dnaA the first gene in the chromosome.\nThe annotation was added by the NCBI Prokaryotic Genome Annotation Pipeline (PGAP). Information about PGAP can be found here: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/genome/annotation_prok/",
